--TEST--
`clone` of a generator the checker cannot see throws the copy walk's `LogicError`, through `mixed` and through `Iterator<T>` alike, and a program's own iterator still clones — `rule:iteration/generator-stays-in-one-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:iteration/generators
class Count {
    public static function oneTwo(): Iterator<int> {
        yield 1;
        yield 2;
    }
}

final class Steps implements Iterator<int> {
    public int $n = 0;
    public function advance(): bool {
        $this->n = $this->n + 1;
        return $this->n < 3;
    }
    public function current(): int {
        return $this->n;
    }
}

mixed $loose = Count::oneTwo();
try {
    var $first = clone $loose;
    echo "cloned through mixed\n";
} catch (LogicError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

Iterator<int> $typed = Count::oneTwo();
try {
    var $second = clone $typed;
    echo "cloned through Iterator<int>\n";
} catch (LogicError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}

// The refused copy did not touch the generator, so it still runs from its start.
foreach ($typed as int $n) {
    echo $n, "\n";
}

Iterator<int> $steps = new Steps();
var $third = clone $steps;
echo "a class of your own still clones\n";
--EXPECT--
clone(): a generator is a suspended frame, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary
clone(): a generator is a suspended frame, so it has no meaning on the other side of a copy boundary
1
2
a class of your own still clones
